{
  "title": "The Two-Sided Legal Order of Capital Accumulation",
  "description": "A schematic of Gonçalves' two-sided framework — capital accumulation as the exchange of equivalents (fetishized legal form, theorized by Pashukanis, visible as legal iconography) and expropriation (explicit legal violence, theorized by Federici, codified in statute) — with a chronology layer plotting each installation on a shared 1480–1920 axis extended back to the late-medieval and early-modern legal-demonological substrate — the *Malleus Maleficarum* (1487) as the founding witch-hunting manual, the *Constitutio Criminalis Carolina* (1532) as the first systematic penal codification of the Holy Roman Empire, the Tudor–Stuart English witch statutes (1563, 1604, 1624), the German mass witch-trials at Bamberg and Würzburg (1626–31), the Hopkins East Anglian trials (1645–47), the Scottish witch-hunt peak (1661–62), the French decriminalization of magic under Louis XIV (1682), Salem (1692), and Ripa's *Iconologia* (1593) as the early-modern anchor for the iconographic vocabulary the 19th-century Justitia and Marianne draw on. The codification band is split into five domain sub-lanes (enclosure, labour, sexuality/household, penal, colonial), with the colonial lane (India, Brazil) foregrounding the double gendering Agnes and Sreenivas identify in colonial personal law. Bridge literatures (Fletcher, Portella & Busk, Ince) work the seam between the two sides. <citations>gj3SectA:1-3,gj3SectA:13,UFspHLhg:2-3,UFspHLhg:7,UFspHLhg:12,ovW4Zzwg:2-3,xgz9lKXA:1,HiRKD7pw:3-5,fBP8zeMg:14,fBP8zeMg:16,fBP8zeMg:20,J28JMywg:0,J0uxJrDg:0-2,uJNuB-4A:7,V_n3UOvg:1,SP8C8xrQ:1-2,SP8C8xrQ:5,ahbppUDw:1-2,3AafpZHA:0-2,e0SrgtyA:0-2,rI-bPrfw:0-8,nOCSCrSg:0-2,gvThKkrQ:0-4,Si-vRwbA:1-4,uqRNTpBA:1-4</citations>",
